<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Dominican Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1966 CE – present · capital Santo Domingo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The present Dominican Republic since 1966, which moved from the authoritarian rule of Joaquín Balaguer to democracy and fast tourism-led growth.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the civil war and US intervention of 1965 ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/third-dominican-republic">Third Dominican Republic</a>, an interim government organised elections in June 1966. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joaquin-balaguer">Joaquín Balaguer</a>, a former aide of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Trujillo</a>, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bosch">Juan Bosch</a> and introduced a new constitution in November 1966.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joaquin-balaguer">Balaguer</a> ruled for twelve years with repression of the left, public works and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> exports. The 1978 election brought a peaceful transfer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-guzman-fernandez">Antonio Guzmán</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a> returned from 1986 to 1996. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonel-fernandez">Leonel Fernández</a> and Danilo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-liberation-party">Dominican Liberation Party</a> then governed for most of the next two decades. Tourism, free trade zones, mining and remittances from Dominicans in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> transformed the economy, making it one of the fastest growing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-abinader">Luis Abinader</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modern-revolutionary-party">Modern Revolutionary Party</a> won the presidency in 2020 and was re-elected in 2024. Relations with neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-haiti">Haiti</a>, migration and inequality remain the central issues of the republic.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dominican-republic.jpg" alt="Dominican Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Juan Pablo Duarte · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dominican_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1966 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santo Domingo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Joaquín Balaguer, Leonel Fernández, Luis Abinader</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fourth Dominican Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=dominican-republic" aria-label="Dominican Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2785 1493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dominican Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3105 1749L3105 1749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3105 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santo Domingo. <a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=dominican-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
